REDO required — the first commit (97e3207/c147371f, reset off-branch) used a FABRICATED artifact and is superseded. product_transport assembled a legacy-shaped TransportPlan from the session AND spliced codegen_seam_facts from a DIFFERENT product (program.transport / MaterializedTransportPlan) onto it — a combined object no production consumer produces or reads, purely to keep ~50 old test bodies unchanged. That violates 'tests use production paths' (a test-only shim) and the ShapeId-is-pure-layout / split-artifact principle, AND forced pulling the full RootBackendProduct (full native build) to get program.transport -> the >1s tiny-fixture builds.

CORRECT APPROACH (the actual 'new information flow', as compiler2_pull_transport_* already do): each test reads the product's GENUINE split surfaces, no reconstructed plan:
- shapes / positions / codegen seams -> program.transport (MaterializedTransportPlan) directly (shape_at(pos), codegen_seam_facts);
- callable / boundary contracts -> session.callable_facts_inventory() / session.boundary_facts_inventory() (+ world.boundary(id));
- membership -> session.demanded_executables().
Rewrite the assertion helpers that take &TransportPlan to take the real product surfaces. Delete the session_transport_plan-assembly-in-tests + the codegen_seam_facts splice. Drive only as far as the transport facts (materialization), NOT the full backend/native — this reads only what's needed, so tiny fixtures derive sub-second (subsumes fz-go4.18.10's 'lighter accessor'; residual >1s on enum_take_drop_split/enum_predicate_search is the separate fz-f98.14 convergence bug). Same ASSERTIONS preserved (still option C, port intent, not weaken).
